  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "Introduction",
      "text": "<p align=\"right\">\n  <strong>English</strong> · <a href=\"./README_ZH.md\">简体中文</a>\n</p>\n\n<p align=\"center\">\n  <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/dsh-provider-usage\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/npm/v/dsh-provider-usage.svg?cacheSeconds=300\" alt=\"npm version\"></a>\n  <a href=\"./LICENSE\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-blue.svg\" alt=\"MIT license\"></a>\n  <img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/DeepSeek%20Harness-plugin-202724\" alt=\"DeepSeek Harness plugin\">\n</p>"
    },
    {
      "section": "dsh-provider-usage",
      "text": "**Every provider's balance, one glance away.** A [DeepSeek Harness](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness) plugin that floats a small draggable usage ball over the Web GUI **and the Desktop app** — no more logging into each provider console to check whether you are about to run out of credit mid-session. One package supports both surfaces: the desktop window renders the same Web GUI, so the same install works everywhere."
    },
    {
      "section": "Features",
      "text": "- **Auto-detection** — enumerates the provider routes registered in the current profile (`ctx.llm`); zero configuration for well-known routes.\n- **Per-kind wire adapters** — routes with no public balance/usage API (Google, Mistral, Groq, Bedrock, Azure, Qwen Token Plan, …) are listed as `unsupported` instead of being silently dropped:"
    },
    {
      "section": "Features",
      "text": "| kind | routes | query | shows |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | `deepseek` | `deepseek-official`, `deepseek` | `GET {baseURL}/user/balance` | total / granted / topped-up balance |\n  | `deepseek-account` | `deepseek-account` (dsh ≥ 0.1.7) | `GET {platformOrigin}/api/v0/users/get_user_summary` | account wallet balance (recharge + bonus merged; **reads the harness's built-in DeepSeek Account sign-in**, zero config). The harness composes this route unconditionally, so the card is **omitted while no sign-in is stored** — it appears on the next poll after you sign in |\n  | `moonshot` | `moonshotai-cn`, `moonshotai` | `GET {baseURL}/users/me/balance` | available / voucher / cash balance |\n  | `kimi-coding` | `kimi-coding` | `GET {baseURL}/v1/usages` | 5h / weekly / monthly usage windows + reset countdown. A lapsed membership is reported as **“no active plan”** instead of a blank card — the endpoint answers `{}` (HTTP 200) once the plan ends |\n  | `openrouter` | `openrouter` | `GET {origin}/api/v1/credits` | credits used / total |\n  | `github-copilot` | `github-copilot` | `GET api.github.com/copilot_internal/user` | plan usage snapshots (paid) or monthly usage (free) |\n  | `openai-codex` | `open"
    },
    {
      "section": "Screenshots",
      "text": "The floating ball (bottom-left, with the green healthy halo) and the open usage panel:\n\n| DeepSeek in use | OpenAI Codex in use |\n| --- | --- |\n|  |  |"
    },
    {
      "section": "Provider setup guides",
      "text": "Most providers need nothing beyond their API key. Two families need one extra setup step:"
    },
    {
      "section": "Provider setup guides",
      "text": "- **OpenAI Codex** — a ChatGPT-subscription provider authenticated by an OAuth grant in the harness credential store (no API key field). Route setup, token refresh/rotation behavior, and the intermittent \"servers overloaded\" retry policy: [docs/provider-codex.md](docs/provider-codex.md).\n- **Volcengine Ark Agent Plan / Coding Plan** — plan quota lives on Volcengine's control plane and needs a signed IAM AK/SK pair in addition to the inference key. Route and credential setup: [docs/provider-ark.md](docs/provider-ark.md)."
    },
    {
      "section": "Install or upgrade",
      "text": "> [!NOTE]\n> Requires an existing [DeepSeek Harness](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness) installation. Install and upgrade are the same command on every surface."
    },
    {
      "section": "Web GUI",
      "text": "After changing the plugin set, restart `dsh web` and refresh the page (later code-only changes need just a rebuild + re-add + refresh). If the release you want was published very recently, your profile's supply-chain cooldown (`minimumReleaseAge`) may silently keep the older version — pin the exact version instead (`dsh plugin --profile web add dsh-provider-usage@0.3.10`) and dsh will exempt it automatically. The version badge in the panel header confirms which release is actually loaded."
    },
    {
      "section": "Desktop app",
      "text": "The same package also runs in the Desktop app — the desktop window renders the same Web GUI, so **no separate plugin or build is needed**. The desktop app owns its own profile (`~/.dsh/profiles/desktop`), so install the plugin there once:"
    },
    {
      "section": "Desktop app",
      "text": "1. In the desktop app, open **Settings → Plugins** → **Add plugin** → **Install a third-party plugin** ([screenshot](docs/desktop-install-menu-en.png)).\n2. Enter `dsh-provider-usage` and click **Install** ([screenshot](docs/desktop-install-dialog-en.png)).\n3. Click **Enable now** ([screenshot](docs/desktop-install-enable-en.png))."
    },
    {
      "section": "Desktop app",
      "text": "The floating ball appears in the desktop window immediately ([screenshot](docs/desktop-panel-en.png)). Panel preferences (ball position, refresh interval, thresholds) persist per origin, so the desktop app and the Web GUI keep their own layouts independently.\n\nTo upgrade on the Desktop app, uninstall then reinstall from the same Plugins page (the desktop dialog asks for this order) and reload the window."
    },
    {
      "section": "Desktop app",
      "text": "CLI alternative: fully quit the desktop app (including the tray), then use the CLI carried by the desktop installation — `<install-dir>\\resources\\runtime\\cli\\bin\\dsh.cmd plugin --profile desktop add dsh-provider-usage@latest`. The npm-installed `dsh` refuses the reserved `desktop` profile (`profile \"desktop\" is managed exclusively by the Electron application`)."
    },
    {
      "section": "Build from source",
      "text": "```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/lizhouai/dsh-provider-usage.git\ncd dsh-provider-usage\npnpm install\npnpm build"
    },
    {
      "section": "Build from source",
      "text": "Install the **tarball**, not the repo directory: `dsh plugin --profile web add .` links the repo, whose own `node_modules` then shadows the harness's shared `@deepseek-ai/cordis` instance and the host half never registers (RPC 404). The link form is still handy for client-only UI iteration — the browser bundle is self-contained, so a rebuild + page refresh picks it up — but switch to the tarball (or the npm release) whenever you need the host half. If pnpm fails with `EPERM ... symlink` while replacing a linked install, delete the stale `node_modules/dsh-provider-usage` junction in the profile directory and retry."
    },
    {
      "section": "Host compatibility",
      "text": "The host packages this plugin imports (`@deepseek-ai/dsh-credentials`, `-settings`, `-typert-protocol`, `-launch-environment`) resolve to the running harness's own copies, and dsh's peer gate compares the declared ranges against the runtime version. This plugin therefore declares an **open lower bound** (`>=0.1.2-alpha.3`), which every runtime passes — a narrow `^`-range would be skipped as `skipping profile bundle` on each dsh minor release. Install through `dsh plugin … add` (pnpm-based): plain `npm install` answers `ERESOLVE`, because standard semver never matches a prerelease against an open range."
    },
    {
      "section": "Host compatibility",
      "text": "If a dsh release renames a seam the plugin reads, the plugin degrades instead of breaking — the harness log names what is missing (`provider-usage: this dsh provides no …`, see `src/host-seams.ts`), and the [`compat`](.github/workflows/compat.yml) workflow replays the gate against the `latest` and `next` release lines daily, so drift surfaces in CI rather than in your panel."
    },
    {
      "section": "Configuration",
      "text": "Defaults work out of the box: the plugin auto-detects every provider route of the active profile. A trusted profile can tune behavior in its bundle patch — `~/.dsh/profiles/web/cordis.patch.yml` (desktop: `~/.dsh/profiles/desktop/cordis.patch.yml`) — **override the bundle's row by id** (the package's own bundle patch already inserts it; a second `insert` of the same id fails the boot with `duplicate loader entry id`):"
    },
    {
      "section": "Configuration",
      "text": "```yaml\n- id: provider-usage\n  name: dsh-provider-usage\n  config:\n    refreshSeconds: 60          # suggested panel refresh interval (5–86400)"
    },
    {
      "section": "Configuration",
      "text": "| Field | Type | Default | Description |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `refreshSeconds` | number | `60` | Suggested widget refresh interval in seconds (5–86400) |\n| `balanceRedThreshold` | number | `10` | Balance below this amount turns red, compared in the balance's own currency |\n| `balanceYellowThreshold` | number | `30` | Balance below this amount turns yellow, compared in the balance's own currency |\n| `autoDetect` | boolean | `true` | Enumerate live provider routes from the llm registry |\n| `queryTimeoutMs` | number | `20000` | Per-attempt query timeout (1000–120000); each retry gets a fresh timeout |\n| `queryRetries` | number | `2` | Retries for transient errors — timeout / network / HTTP 408, 425, 429, 5xx (0–10); permanent 4xx errors fail immediately |\n| `queryRetryDelayMs` | number | `2000` | Base delay between retries (100–60000), doubled per attempt, capped at 10s |\n| `providers` | array | `[]` | Manual provider specs: `{id, kind, baseURL, apiKeyEnv, displayName?, enabled?}`; `kind` is one of the adapter table above |"
    },
    {
      "section": "Configuration",
      "text": "The same fields can be hot-updated under the `provider-usage:` namespace in `~/.dsh/settings.yaml`."
    },
    {
      "section": "Adding a manual provider",
      "text": "```yaml\nconfig:\n  providers:\n    - id: my-deepseek-gateway\n      kind: deepseek"
    },
    {
      "section": "How it works",
      "text": "- **Host half** (`src/index.ts`): `UsageService extends TypertRemoteService` exposes `usage/list` via the `Remote('list')` marker, applied without decorator syntax (SRC mode, no codegen). Config is declared with schemastery, and the settings provider's `installSection` enables hot updates from the settings document.\n- **Client half** (`src/client/`): a `window.__ModuleLoader__.load({id, factory})` bundle (built by tsdown) mounts through the `sidebar.footer.action` slot (used purely as a mount point — the trigger itself is a floating ball portaled to `document.body`) and polls `usage/list` through `ctx.connection.rpc.call('/api', 'usage/list', {args:{}})` on its own interval. The service stays stateless — every poll fetches live values."
    },
    {
